## Subject: `xhpl` (variant `cpu-mpi`)

**What it builds:** A single MPI executable `xhpl` (`testing/Makefile.am:6`) that solves a random dense double-precision linear system via LU factorization — the classic High-Performance Linpack benchmark (HPL 2.3). It links against an external BLAS library (OpenBLAS/MKL/ATLAS/ESSL, resolved via `dgemm_` in `configure.ac:31` / `acinclude.m4`) and requires an MPI implementation (`README:12-14`).

**Parallelism & communication:**
- **MPI process parallelism** only in the source itself. Ranks are laid out on a logical **P×Q 2D process grid** (`HPL.dat` Ps/Qs, `HPL_pddriver.c`).
- **Point-to-point model** — HPL builds its *own* broadcasts and reductions on top of `MPI_Send`/`MPI_Recv`/`MPI_Irecv`; there are **no native MPI collectives** in the source.
- **Pattern set:** `neighbor` (panel roll/spread of the U matrix between grid neighbors, `HPL_rollT.c`), `broadcast` (ring/tree panel broadcast along rows, `HPL_1ring.c`), and `reduction` (pivot-max / residual via `HPL_all_reduce`, `HPL_pdtest.c`).
- Both **latency-sensitive** (small frequent pivot reductions, 0-byte syncs) and **bandwidth-sensitive** (large `lengthR*LDU` double panel transfers).

**Memory:** **Compute-bound** with **high arithmetic intensity** — dominated by cache-blocked BLAS3 `dgemm` (O(N³) flops / O(N²) data, NB-blocked → good cache reuse). This is the field that most distinguishes HPL from a memory-bound stencil code. No shared memory, no affinity pinning in the source.

**Accelerator/I/O:** None — pure CPU code; no CUDA/HIP/SYCL, no parallel I/O (only `fopen` of HPL.dat/HPL.out), no checkpoint/restart.

**Launch/topology:** Started with `mpirun -np <P*Q> ./xhpl` (docs note starting on ≥16 nodes for a 6×8/2×8 grid example, `TUNING:126-130`). Judged **multi_node**, **pack** placement, co-location **preferred** for interconnect bandwidth — but not *required*, since there's no shared-memory dependency. I recorded no concrete launch counts because the repo only gives them as illustrative examples, not a single real invocation.

Only one meaningful build variant exists at the source level (CPU + MPI + external BLAS), so a single trace `xhpl:cpu-mpi` captures it. (Threaded BLAS would add intra-rank threading, but that comes from the linked library, not this code.)
